@node Overview
@chapter Overview

A decimal stamp such as @samp{14301.4564} names a moment: day 14301,
0.4564 of the way through it.  A duration is a fraction of a day, so
90 minutes is @samp{.0625} and a working day of eight hours is
@samp{.3333}.  Durations add as plain decimals.

@example
$ daykeep .9 - .1
.2000
$ daykeep --hm '09:00 - 10:30' 11:00 - 11:45
1h 30m
2h 15m
$ daykeep --now
14301.4564
@end example

daykeep also reads @samp{HH:MM} wall-clock times, so logs written for
timekeep, the program it grew out of, still sum.  Hours and minutes can
be printed as well (@pxref{Summing ranges}).

@node Decimal time
@chapter Decimal time

@menu
* The epoch::           Day 0 and why it is in UTC.
* Units::               What the decimals mean.
* Printed forms::       How stamps and durations are printed.
@end menu

@node The epoch
@section The epoch

@cindex epoch
@cindex day 0
Day 0 is 1987-07-28, and it starts at 00:00 UTC.  Day numbers count
whole days from there: day 1 is 1987-07-29, and day 14301 is
2026-09-22.  Days before the epoch have negative numbers.  In terms of
Unix time:

@example
stamp = unix_seconds / 86400 - 6417
@end example

@noindent
since 1987-07-28 is 6417 days after 1970-01-01.  You can check the
current stamp against @command{date} and @command{bc}:

@example
$ echo "scale=4; ($(date -u +%s) - $(date -u -d 1987-07-28 +%s)) / 86400" | bc
14301.4564
$ daykeep --now
14301.4564
@end example

@cindex UTC
@cindex time zone
A day begins at midnight UTC, not at local midnight, so a stamp names
the same moment everywhere and never skips or repeats around daylight
saving changes.  The price is that a decimal day does not line up with
your local day.  West of Greenwich a new day number begins in the
evening (at 21:00 in UTC@minus{}3), and east of it one begins in the
morning.  Work that runs past that point ends on the next day number,
and daykeep handles that as it handles work past local midnight
(@pxref{Ranges}).

@node Units
@section Units

daykeep keeps every value as a whole number of seconds and rounds only
when it prints, so the totals of long logs do not drift.  It prints
four decimal places.  One step in the last place is 8.64 seconds, and
some common durations are:

@multitable {15 minutes} {1.0000} {no (.000694@dots{})}
@headitem Duration @tab Decimal @tab Exact?
@item 1 minute    @tab .0007 @tab no (.000694@dots{})
@item 15 minutes  @tab .0104 @tab no (.010416@dots{})
@item 1 hour      @tab .0417 @tab no (.041666@dots{})
@item 90 minutes  @tab .0625 @tab yes
@item 3 hours     @tab .1250 @tab yes
@item 6 hours     @tab .2500 @tab yes
@item 8 hours     @tab .3333 @tab no (.333333@dots{})
@item 1 day       @tab 1.0000 @tab yes
@end multitable

A duration of @var{n} minutes is @var{n}/1440 day.  Whole multiples
of 18 minutes (@samp{.0125}) print exactly, as 90 minutes and 3 hours
do.  Other durations are rounded to the nearest step, half up.

@node Printed forms
@section Printed forms

@itemize
@item
A stamp is printed with a day number of at least five digits and four
decimals: @samp{14301.4564}, @samp{00007.0000}.  Every stamp from day
0 on reads back as exactly that stamp, without completion (@pxref{Stamps}).
A moment before the epoch is printed as its negative day number plus
the fraction into that day: @samp{-0001.1250} is 03:00 UTC on day
@minus{}1, 1987-07-27.  Such stamps are printed but not read.

@item
A duration under one day is printed without a leading zero:
@samp{.0625}.  Longer ones print the whole days: @samp{1.2500}.

@item
@option{--hm} prints durations as @samp{1h 30m}, and
@option{--format=minutes} as a whole number of minutes, @samp{90}.
@end itemize

@node Stamps
@chapter Stamps

@cindex stamp
@cindex completion
A stamp is written @samp{@var{day}.@var{fraction}}, and either side may
be short.  daykeep fills in what is missing.

@table @asis
@item Missing right digits
are zeros.  @samp{.5} is @samp{.5000}, and @samp{14301} is
@samp{14301.0000}.

@item Missing left digits
are taken from today.  The digits you give are the last digits of the
day number, and daykeep picks the @emph{most recent} day, up to today,
whose number ends in them.  With no day digits at all, the day is
today.

@item A leading @samp{+}
picks the @emph{first} day from today on that ends in those digits
instead.
@end table

With today being day 14301:

@multitable {@samp{+301.25}} {14301.2500}
@headitem Typed @tab Means
@item @samp{.5}       @tab 14301.5000
@item @samp{301}      @tab 14301.0000
@item @samp{9}        @tab 14299.0000
@item @samp{302}      @tab 13302.0000
@item @samp{+9}       @tab 14309.0000
@item @samp{+301.25}  @tab 14301.2500
@item @samp{+302}     @tab 14302.0000
@item @samp{14250.1}  @tab 14250.1000
@end multitable

A stamp with as many day digits as today's number, or more, is taken
literally, so a printed stamp reads back as itself.

@cindex HH:MM
@cindex local time
A stamp may also be written @samp{HH:MM}, meaning that time on your
local clock today, as set by @env{TZ}.  With @option{-u} it is read
in UTC instead.

@node Ranges
@chapter Ranges

@cindex range
A range is written @samp{@var{start} - @var{end}}.  The @samp{-} is
required; spaces around it are optional.

@var{start} is completed from today, as any stamp (@pxref{Stamps}).
@var{end} is completed from @var{start}: when it leaves out day digits,
it is the first matching time that is not before @var{start}.  So an
entry that runs past the start of a day number needs no day digits:

@example
$ daykeep .9 - .1
.2000
$ daykeep 14301.375 - .4375
.0625
$ daykeep --hm 23:24 - 00:52
1h 28m
@end example

@noindent
In the first example, @samp{.1} would fall before @samp{.9} on the same
day, so it rolls on to the next day.  An @var{end} with a full day
number is taken literally, and it is an error for it to fall before
@var{start}.

A range whose @var{end} is missing, @samp{@var{start} -} at the end of a
line, is still open.  It runs until now, which is how an entry that the
tracker is still timing is summed.

@node Summing ranges
@chapter Summing ranges

With no mode option, daykeep sums ranges.  They come from:

@enumerate
@item
the operands, joined by spaces, so a range may span several of them
(@samp{daykeep 10:00 - 11:00} works); then
@item
each @option{-f @var{file}}, in order (@samp{-} is standard input);
@item
standard input, if there are neither operands nor files.
@end enumerate

A line may hold several ranges.  After each range daykeep prints the
running total.  With @option{-s} it prints only the final total, even
if there were no ranges at all.

@example
$ cat today.log
14301.3750 - 14301.4375
14301.4583 - 14301.5000
$ daykeep -f today.log
.0625
.1042
$ daykeep -s --hm < today.log
2h 30m
@end example

@cindex errors
A line that cannot be read is reported on standard error, prefixed with
@samp{@var{file}:@var{line}:} when it came from a file, and the rest of
that line is skipped.  Summing goes on, and daykeep exits with status 1
at the end.

@node Now and convert
@chapter Now and convert

@option{--now} prints the current stamp.

@option{--convert} (@option{-c}) converts each @var{value}:

@itemize
@item
A decimal stamp becomes a local date and time, in a form that
@option{--convert} reads back:
@example
$ TZ=America/Sao_Paulo daykeep -c 14301.5
2026-09-22 09:00:00 -0300
@end example

@item
@samp{HH:MM}, or an ISO date
@samp{YYYY-MM-DD[ HH:MM[:SS]][ +HHMM]}, becomes a stamp.  A date with
no offset is local time.
@example
$ daykeep -c 1987-07-28 '2026-09-22 12:00 +0000'
00000.1250
14301.5000
@end example
@end itemize

@noindent
(The first result is for a zone at UTC@minus{}3, where local midnight
is 03:00 UTC.)  With @option{-u}, dates and times are read and printed
in UTC.

@node Tracker
@chapter Tracker

@cindex tracker
@option{--track} (@option{-t}) times your work interactively.  It shows
a table of entries and a running total:

@example
$ daykeep -t -a work.log
 1  14301.3750 - 14301.4375   .0625
 2  14301.4564 - ...          (running)
total .0625
[enter] stamp  [x] remove last  [e] edit last  [s] save  [q] quit
@end example

@table @asis
@item @key{RET}
Stamp the current time.  Stamps start and end entries in turn.
@item @kbd{x}
Remove the last stamp.
@item @kbd{e}
Edit the last stamp.  It takes any stamp form (@samp{.4},
@samp{14:30}, @samp{14301.4}).  The first stamp is completed from
today, and later ones from the stamp before, as a range @var{end} is,
so @samp{.9} then @samp{.1} ends on the next day.
@item @kbd{s}
Save the log (it is kept current anyway, see below).
@item @kbd{q}, @kbd{C-c}, @kbd{C-d}
Quit.
@end table

@cindex log
With @option{-a @var{log}}, the session's entries are kept in
@var{log}, one range per line, as @samp{@var{start} - @var{end}}, or
@samp{@var{start} -} while an entry is open.  daykeep rewrites the
session's lines after every change, so the file is always current: a
signal or a crash loses nothing.  Lines that were in @var{log} before
the session are never touched.

If @var{log} ends in an open entry, the next @samp{daykeep -t -a
@var{log}} resumes it.  Only one tracker at a time can use a given
@var{log}.  Because the lines are ranges, @samp{daykeep -f @var{log}}
sums the log, open entry included.

Without @option{-a}, nothing is written to disk.

@node Invoking daykeep
@chapter Invoking daykeep

@example
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} [@var{range}]@dots{}
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --now
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --convert @var{value}@dots{}
daykeep [@var{option}]@dots{} --track
@end example

@table @option
@item -f @var{file}
@itemx --file=@var{file}
Read ranges from @var{file}; @samp{-} is standard input.  May be given
more than once.

@item -s
@itemx --summarize
Print only the final total.

@item --format=@var{fmt}
Print durations as @var{fmt}: @samp{decimal} (@samp{.0625}, the
default), @samp{hm} (@samp{1h 30m}) or @samp{minutes} (@samp{90}).

@item --hm
Same as @option{--format=hm}.

@item --now
Print the current stamp.

@item -c
@itemx --convert
Convert each operand (@pxref{Now and convert}).

@item -t
@itemx --track
Track time interactively (@pxref{Tracker}).

@item -a @var{log}
@itemx --append=@var{log}
With @option{--track}, keep the entries in @var{log}.

@item -u
@itemx --utc
Read and print wall-clock times in UTC instead of local time.

@item --help
Print a usage message and exit.

@item --version
Print version information and exit.
@end table

Options may be mixed with operands unless @env{POSIXLY_CORRECT} is set,
and @samp{--} ends the options.

@section Exit status

@table @asis
@item 0
All went well.
@item 1
Some input was invalid; the rest was still processed.
@item 2
A usage error, or an I/O error (including a failed write to standard
output).
@end table

@section Environment

@table @env
@item TZ
The time zone for @samp{HH:MM} and for dates, unless @option{-u} is
given.
@item POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, options must come before operands.
@item DAYKEEP_NOW
If set, used as the current time: a full stamp such as
@samp{14301.4564}, or @samp{@@@var{seconds}} since the Unix epoch.  It
is meant for tests.
@end table

@node Other tools
@chapter Using daykeep with other tools

Decimal output is plain numbers, so the usual tools can work with it.

@itemize
@item
Sum several logs in minutes with @command{awk}:
@example
$ for f in *.log; do daykeep -s --format=minutes -f "$f"; done |
  awk '@{ m += $1 @} END @{ printf "%dh %02dm\n", m / 60, m % 60 @}'
@end example

@item
Show today's total in a tmux status line, or mail it from
@command{cron}:
@example
set -g status-right '#(daykeep -s -f ~/work.log)'
@end example

@item
Check a stamp against @command{date}:
@example
$ date -d @@$(( (14301 + 6417) * 86400 ))
@end example
@end itemize

@bye
